     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is about twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day (2,000 mg/day for extended-release). A single dose of 1,700 mg is also well above the usual per-dose limit of about 1,000 mg. Doses this high raise the risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis. The patient's normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that.\n\n2. **You're not a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, and a pharmacist normally reviews them too.\n\nThe dose may be a typo. For example, it could be meant as 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg twice daily. I can't assume what was intended.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. Once I have a verified order, I'll enter it right away. If the prescriber confirms a corrected dose within the standard range, I can enter that."
      }
     ],
